# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Pull from a pre-signed URL is a way for authorised users to collect processed
files through the Integration Hub file-transfer portal. When a matching clean
file is ready, the Integration Hub makes a separate copy in a private,
encrypted pickup bucket and sends a Slack notification with a link to the
portal.

Use this pattern when your users can collect files through the authenticated
portal and you do not want to operate an SFTP or FTPS server to receive them.

## How the pattern works

![Diagram showing how users collect processed files through the Integration Hub file-transfer portal.](/images/supported-patterns-pull-from-presigned-url.png)

When a file matches a configured source prefix and Slack notification, the
Integration Hub copies the exact clean file version to a recipient-specific
location in the pickup bucket. The copy is encrypted separately from the clean
file, and the original remains unchanged.

The Integration Hub sends a Slack notification with a link to the file-transfer
portal and the encoded path to the pickup file. Users sign in with their
organisational account and collect the file through the portal. Access is
read-only and limited to the recipient's pickup directory.

Pickup files expire after seven days, so authorised users should collect them within that period.

## What your service receives

Your nominated users receive a Slack notification when a file is ready, with a
portal link and the file's pickup path. They need to be members of an
authorised group configured for the pickup directory.

This pattern does not provide your service with a pre-signed S3 URL, S3
credentials, or direct bucket access. Collection takes place through the
authenticated file-transfer portal.

## Discuss this pattern

This pattern is still in design. To discuss whether it is suitable for your
service, post in `#ask-integration-hub` with:

* the service that will send files to the Integration Hub
* the service and users that will collect the files
* the source prefix and Slack notification you want to use
* the environment you need, such as development or production
* your expected file volume, size and collection frequency
* your data-retention requirements

Do not include passwords, private keys, file contents or other sensitive data.

## Getting help

For a question about this draft pattern, post in `#ask-integration-hub`.
Include the services involved and the environment you are planning for.
